00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.333
Most people have four or five pictures of their AI influencer and no idea how to get from there to a real dataset.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:13.383
And in this video, I show you the exact system we use to turn a handful of pictures into a complete, training-ready-to-use dataset.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.017
No matter if you have five Instagram shots or just one single face picture.

00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:21.033
And the dataset does not care what you train it on later. That part is completely up to you.

00:00:22.117 --> 00:00:27.250
And everything we see today, the full prompt system and the ComfyUI workflow, you get complete for free at the end of this video.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.657
Okay, let's get straight into it. This is Lina, our AI influencer for this video.

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:35.307
And these four pictures here are everything we start with.

00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:38.690
One clean, one clean studio shot of her face and three random phone pictures.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.223
And if you have an AI, and if you have an AI influencer, you probably have exactly that.

00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.597
And whatever you build with her later, it all comes out of one folder.

00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:47.842
But four pictures are not the data set.

00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:50.157
Here is the part almost nobody talks about.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.919
You never generate everything out of these four pictures.

00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:56.747
You start with the face, and every step after that you use what you just made.

00:00:56.883 --> 00:01:01.090
This is a finished dataset, and this is not a folder with nice pictures of her,

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.373
this is one whole person from every side. The first part here is their face from every angle,

00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:09.773
and these are not nice portraits, this is the geometry of their face,

00:01:09.783 --> 00:01:12.073
in every position a camera can stand in. And these are her expressions.

00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:17.173
And these are her expressions, smiling, mouth open, eyes closed, and looking away.

00:01:17.183 --> 00:01:22.060
And then comes her body, upper body from every side, and after that the full body from every side.

00:01:22.383 --> 00:01:25.720
And at the end comes the — and at the end comes the real-life pics, just normal pictures of her

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:29.942
in a real room, to add the hyper — to add the hyper-realistic Instagram effect to the dataset.

00:01:30.083 --> 00:01:37.987
and if one of these — and if one of these parts is missing, you will feel it later,

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:42.507
in every image you ever make with her. And you don't need hundreds of pictures for this,

00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:46.870
what matters is that every part is covered. And if you train a LoRA on this later,

00:01:46.883 --> 00:01:51.140
25 to 36 good ones beat 75 messy ones every time.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:55.707
And before we go into the prompts, one quick thing. Everything in this video is free,

00:01:55.717 --> 00:01:59.670
and at the end you get the whole CompTIA workflow and prompt system on top,

00:01:59.717 --> 00:02:02.863
so the only thing I want from you is a like and a nice comment.

00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:07.226
And if you are really serious about building AI influencers and you want the whole path,

00:02:07.236 --> 00:02:11.257
not just one piece of it, then come over to our Skool community.

00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:14.263
That is where we go way deeper than I can ever go here on YouTube.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:18.263
Okay, now every picture in the data set comes from one prompt, and there's one

00:02:18.273 --> 00:02:22.173
rule for all of them. Get this one wrong, and nothing else will save you. There are

00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:25.947
these are, these are the prompts for her face, and I want you to look one thing.

00:02:25.957 --> 00:02:30.423
Look at her hair color. There is none. Not in this one, and not in any of the

00:02:30.433 --> 00:02:34.950
prompts. Because identity never goes into the prompt. These pictures say who she is,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.307
the prompt only says where she is, and how it is shot. The moment you write

00:02:38.317 --> 00:02:39.539
blonde, the moment you write blonde hair into a prompt, the moment you write blonde

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:43.787
hair into a prompt, that prompt only works for blondes. Leave it out, and the

00:02:43.797 --> 00:02:47.590
same prompt builds a data set for every character you will ever make. And there's

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.949
and there's exactly one exception, and there's exactly one exception, and you

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:54.803
can see it right here. Keep the same eye color as in the reference images. This is

00:02:54.813 --> 00:02:58.190
not a description. That points back at your own pictures. So models drift,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.223
some models drift by the eye color, so we lock it in. And that is the difference

00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:03.603
that keeps it safe, and that is the difference that keeps it safe for every

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:07.686
AI influencer. Because the look never says what her eyes look like, it only

00:03:07.696 --> 00:03:08.973
says don't change them.

00:03:08.983 --> 00:03:12.543
And everything here comes out of that one studio picture of her face.

00:03:12.733 --> 00:03:18.010
So if you don't have one yet, that is the first thing you make, and you make clean with nothing

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.207
else in the frame. Now let's look at her face, because her face has to teach two things.

00:03:19.217 --> 00:03:21.617
The first one are the angles, so she is — now, now let's look at her face, because her face has

00:03:21.627 --> 00:03:26.673
to teach two things. The first one are the angles, so who she is from every side, and, and her,

00:03:26.683 --> 00:03:31.046
and there, and there her face stays neutral, because only the camera moves. And the second

00:03:31.056 --> 00:03:36.059
one are her expressions, so what her face can actually do. And there the camera stands front

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:40.690
out, because now only her face moves. But never both in the same picture,

00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:44.253
because you have to be able to see what changed between the shots.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.679
If the camera moves and her face moves at the same time,

00:03:47.817 --> 00:03:52.920
you cannot tell if the mouse looks different because of the angle or because she is smiling.

00:03:53.117 --> 00:03:54.192
And one quick tip on the background. Take gray and not white, because white flattens the skin,

00:03:54.202 --> 00:04:00.507
and that is where plastic look starts. And there's one more thing, but that one does not go

00:04:00.517 --> 00:04:04.507
into the data set. That is one single picture with all her expressions in a grid, and we only build

00:04:04.517 --> 00:04:09.480
that when we want to make videos with her. So the video model has her whole range in one reference.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:18.982
And that is her face done, and these pictures are now our references for the body.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:23.840
Now her body. And this is where it gets interesting. And Lina already has body pictures.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:29.257
But if all you have is a face, then there is no body yet. And the image model just invents one.

00:04:29.433 --> 00:04:34.527
And here comes the most important part of this whole video. The first body picture you accept

00:04:34.537 --> 00:04:39.869
is her body from now on. Everything after that has to match it. So take your time with this one.

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:44.406
And from there we — and from there we log it in. Same proportion as in the reference pictures

00:04:44.416 --> 00:04:49.697
in every single prompt. Because a lot of models make her smaller or bigger on their own without

00:04:49.707 --> 00:04:55.043
asking. And sometimes the model still does its own thing and then you just run it one more time.

00:04:55.053 --> 00:05:00.819
And that same log also works the other way around. So you can make her curvier or slimmer

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:04.939
or a lot more than that. But this is a thing for our Skool community,

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.839
not for YouTube if you know what I mean.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:12.420
Then we turn her all the way around. From the front to the back. And the outfit stays the same

00:05:12.583 --> 00:05:17.313
the whole way. Because otherwise it's not a rotation, it is just five different photos.

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:22.147
And then the same position again, only frame closer. Because later you will want her in a

00:05:22.157 --> 00:05:27.912
full body shot and in a tight one. And she has to hold up in both. And that is her body done.

00:05:27.983 --> 00:05:30.957
